{"reference":"Hebrews 3","book":"Hebrews","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Moses and the House","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The comparison with Moses begins by honoring him: Moses is faithful in God’s house. The chapter then distinguishes Jesus as the Son over the house, using house-and-builder imagery to make the distinction without denigrating Moses or Judaism.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 3:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_003_b2ed25dfda7118143750151d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Communal Warning","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The repeated appeal to “Today” and the wilderness warning address the community’s shared responsiveness. One interpretive caution is to distinguish this homiletic warning from later, more individualized ideas about apostasy; the passage urges mutual exhortation so that no one is hardened by sin’s deceitfulness.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 3:1-6","Hebrews 3:7-19"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_003_0594ba790247bccf6bd423ea"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Rest Theme Continues","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The warning about entering God’s rest continues into the following passage, where it is developed through the wilderness generation, Psalm 95, creation, Sabbath, Joshua, present access, and future promise. This means that “rest” in Hebrews 3–4 should not be reduced to a single meaning. Hebrews’ use of the exodus and wilderness traditions is a later canonical reuse and should be read without displacing Exodus’s own focus on the LORD’s deliverance and covenant relationship with Israel.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_003_24937e9f5f16b901beb02cd0","reader_path_hebrews_003_dabe35d7c003079cf0b7d02d"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}